Q: Best way to build dynamic query involving an optional limit? A: <p>I was looking for some less verbose approach that somehow works with <code>null</code> on the input. In my case, <code>null</code> is the empty default, rather than <code>Integer.MAX_VALUE</code>. The most intuitive approach was however deprecated by the main contributor:</p>
<pre class="lang-java prettyprint-override"><code>var query = dslContext.select(selectFields)
    .from(derivedTable)
    .where(conditions)
    .orderBy(orderForward)
    .limit(myNullableLimit) // null deprecated by Lukas Eder!
    .getQuery();
</code></pre>
<p>This might have worked back when @Gili was testing it out, and it might still work today, but the implementation may change and it could break the code.</p>
<hr />
<p>OK. So, I'd like to write it in some way that is clear and not very verbose. I'm not very fond of using the <code>DSL.noField(INTEGER)</code>, because it could confuse my teammates and they could end up digging down the rabbit hole like I am right now.</p>
<p>I noticed that any <code>Field</code> seems to be supported. I noticed <code>DSL</code> methods <code>val</code>, <code>inline</code> etc. The manual doesn't mention them, and JavaDoc specifies absolutely nothing on what is expected to be on the input (it doesn't even mention if <code>null</code> is a valid input or not). So, I went digging into the code, trying to learn the truth the hard way.</p>
<p>Then in <code>Limit.java</code> I found out there is a special case for <code>instanceof NoField</code> which skips setting the limit (or offset) value entirely. So, using some other <code>Field</code> (other than <code>val</code> mentioned in documentation and possibly <code>inline</code> used internally) might not be as safe as I would assume from the type being expected as function parameter. Or, at very least, it could cause behavior that differs from what I expected.</p>
<p>All right, so if I want to avoid the whole <code>noField</code>, to prevent confusion of my teammates, I might as well just consider the solution @Gili mentioned in question to be the best, I only need to deal with the different output type. Perhaps just using <code>var</code> will do? It breaks the method chaining, making the result look a little ugly, but I can live with that.</p>
<pre class="lang-java prettyprint-override"><code>var baseQuery = dslContext.select(selectFields)
    .from(derivedTable)
    .where(conditions)
    .orderBy(orderForward);
var withLimit = (length == Integer.MAX_VALUE)
        ? baseQuery : baseQuery.limit(length);
var query = withLimit.getQuery();
</code></pre>
<p>Nice, intuitive, perhaps a little unappealing, and ... wrong. It works if you don't need to apply <code>offset</code> separately. But if you do, there is no simple way.</p>
<hr />
<p><strong>The whole DSL is designed for method chaining.</strong> It uses a lot of different interfaces to represent the current state of your query-writing. This makes it harder to write a bad query.</p>
<p>It also means that after applying <code>limit</code>, you are in a different state than when you haven't done that yet. Both states can still both set <code>offset</code>, but the closest ancestor they extend is <code>SelectForUpdateStep</code>, which is bad news: It doesn't allow <code>offset</code> anymore.</p>
<p>By going for a common ancestor, you inevitably lose some state information, which means fewer clauses are available to you. This could be partially mitigated by jOOQ developers by introducing more interfaces, but it's non-trivial amount of work on what is a Cartesian product of a lot of SQL clauses. And some state information would still be lost.</p>
<p>If I was unable or very stubborn and unwilling to use the <code>NoField</code> approach, I would need this kind of monstrosity to be able to apply both limit and offset:</p>
<pre class="lang-java prettyprint-override"><code>var baseQuery = dslContext.select(selectFields)
    .from(derivedTable)
    .where(conditions)
    .orderBy(orderForward);
SelectForUpdateStep&lt;Record&gt; lastStep;
boolean hasLimit = limit != Integer.MAX_VALUE;
boolean hasOffset = offset != 0;
if (hasLimit &amp;&amp; hasOffset)
    lastStep = baseQuery.limit(offset, limit);
else if (hasLimit)
    lastStep = baseQuery.limit(limit);
else if (hasOffset)
    lastStep = baseQuery.offset(offset);
else
    lastStep = baseQuery;
var query = lastStep.getQuery();
</code></pre>
<p>Looks ugly enough for me to consider just using the <code>DSL.noField(INTEGER)</code> and hoping that my teammates don't get confused (or possibly, never look there).</p>
<p>So, the answer by Lukas Eder is, sadly, the only way to avoid an ugly solution like this one. If only there was a method that expects a <code>@Nullable Number</code> or possibly <code>Optional&lt;Number&gt;</code>, and uses the default when <code>null</code> value is set. The current solution is somewhat counter-intuitive.</p>
